UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "disposition" = 'EXHAUSTED', "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING, BUT IT IS NO LONGER A HYPOTHESIS — the record is now verified and the staged description was WRONG ON THE AUTHOR. Batch 17 staged it from REF-00980 deposited reference list as "Walter F." Open Library OL4653562M (retrieved this session) gives: "Four architectural movement studies for the wheelchair and ambulant disabled", by_statement "[studies directed by] Felix Walter.", published by the DISABLED LIVING FOUNDATION, 346 Kensington High St, London, 1971, 60 pp. R15 says correct an over-claim rather than let a guess harden: the CORPORATE author is the Disabled Living Foundation and Walter directed the studies. Neither the reference list nor the catalogue is wrong; they name different things, and the staged row named only one. WHAT THE FULL TEXT WOULD SETTLE, AND WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN ITS TIER: REF-01005 page 163 reports that Walter and Steinfeld INDEPENDENTLY found 1:16 over 20 feet accessible to at least 95 percent of wheelchair users. That convergence is currently held on Steinfeld testimony alone, so Walter is a single document counted once, not twice. Retrieving it would make the convergence real. NOT RETRIEVED: no digitised copy found via Open Library, Internet Archive or Google Books; HathiTrust returned a Cloudflare interstitial and is unresolved rather than negative. NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED: Jisc Library Hub Discover, and the Disabled Living Foundation successor organisation. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW RETRIEVAL CLAIM, same defect as candidate 110. The re-description above says "no digitised copy found via Open Library, Internet Archive or Google Books". GOOGLE BOOKS WAS NEVER ASKED — HTTP 429, daily quota exceeded. Open Library and Internet Archive stand and were re-derived. The bibliographic record, which is what this row is FOR, is unaffected: Open Library OL4653562M, Disabled Living Foundation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 UPDATE: THE NAMED NEXT ROUTE IS NOW CLOSED, AND THE ROW GAINS THE HOLDINGS LAYER THAT MAKES DOCUMENT DELIVERY POSSIBLE. This row''s previous re-description ended ''NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED: Jisc Library Hub Discover, and the Disabled Living Foundation successor organisation.'' JISC WAS TRIED THIS BATCH AND IS UNREACHABLE: two queries, both HTTP 403 carrying a Cloudflare ''Just a moment...'' interstitial, both persisted. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT JISC HOLDS -- blocked route, not a null -- and it is the second discovery catalogue in two batches to fail this way after HathiTrust. The row should stop naming Jisc as its next step. NOTHING IS RETRACTED FROM THE EARLIER DESCRIPTION: I checked whether batch 18 had missed the Open Library record before writing anything, and it had not -- Felix Walter, DLF, London, 1971, 60 pp is already correctly recorded above, and the corporate-versus-directing-author distinction it draws stands. WHAT IS NEW IS THE IDENTIFIER SET, from the same Open Library edition record OL4653562M re-read this batch: ISBN-10 0901908053, LCCN 77502052, OCLC 222208, LC classification NA2545.P5 D5, Dewey 729/.2, one edition only, publisher address 346 Kensington High St, London W.14, source record marc:marc_loc_2016 -- a LIBRARY OF CONGRESS MARC record, which is why the 1971 date is authoritative rather than inferred, and which independently corroborates the year REF-01005''s Table 13 footnote c gives while that bibliography entry''s own year digits are OCR-damaged. WHY THE IDENTIFIERS ARE THE POINT: GAP-026 says what this needs is a library or document-delivery route and that this is ''a different kind of work from a retrieval ladder''. An OCLC number and an LCCN are exactly what converts that from a search into a request -- interlibrary loan on OCLC 222208, or a direct approach to a holding library. NO SEARCH THIS ENVIRONMENT CAN RUN WILL PRODUCE THE TEXT, and this row should not be handed to another retrieval battery. A NEW SEARCH ROUTE DID WORK AND IS WORTH KEEPING AS METHOD: batch 18 searched Open Library by TITLE and got 0; searching by PUBLISHER, publisher:''Disabled Living Foundation'', returns 99 records and the target is among them. Query shape, not index coverage (R14). STILL THE SAME REASON TO WANT IT: the 1:16-over-20-feet convergence is held on Steinfeld''s testimony about Walter, so it is one document counted once until Walter itself is read. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, AND NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, applied literally: ''if we can''t access something from anywhere then we have to give up on it for now.'' Full text of Walter 1971 is not reachable from anywhere this project can go. ROUTES RECORDED AS TRIED, in this row and in batches 17-19''s retrieval logs: Open Library (the bibliographic record OL4653562M, no digitised copy; by title 0, by publisher 99 with the record among them); Internet Archive (no copy); HathiTrust (Cloudflare interstitial); Jisc Library Hub Discover (HTTP 403, Cloudflare, twice); Google Books (quota refused, never asked; quota value 0). The 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports re-checking every legitimate self-service route and finding none that works; its requests were not persisted in this repository''s retrieval log, so they are not itemised here. THE EARLIER NEXT STEP IS WITHDRAWN: batch 19 closed this row by naming a request-based route with the catalogue identifiers it had found; under the ruling that route is not a task for any person, human or agent, and nothing in this row should be read as one. THE BIBLIOGRAPHIC RECORD STANDS (Disabled Living Foundation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter). WHAT THE PROJECT HOLDS OF WALTER INSTEAD: two independent second-hand accounts -- REF-01005 page 163 (extraction 54) and, new this batch, REF-01008 printed page 4 (extraction 64): an adjustable indoor laboratory ramp, 20 ft at 1:8.6 to 1:16 and 10 ft at 1:5.5 to 1:10, four-point rating scale, recommending for self-propelled wheelchairs 1:9 over 10 ft and 1:16 over 20 ft. Two readers agreeing about Walter is still Walter counted once. Left PENDING-VERIFICATION rather than retired because the column vocabulary has no ''exhausted'' value; the disposition should be read with this note. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): DISPOSITION MOVED TO EXHAUSTED. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, recorded on contact (CLAUDE.md rule 0): a real, CHECK-permitted value for ''exhausted, given up for now'' was added (schema migration 096) and applies to this row, to which no route exists from anywhere. The routes tried and the reasons are in the re-description above; this changes the typed column so that no sweep reads PENDING-VERIFICATION as work. Not queued for anyone.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 109;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "disposition" = 'EXHAUSTED', "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING AND ITS DESCRIPTION IS NOW CORROBORATED BY A PRIMARY SOURCE RATHER THAN BY A REFERENCE LIST. Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0. BUT REF-01005 page 163 characterises it directly: "Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users. However, his sample was taken from wheelchair users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education center and the findings probably reflect the high standard of excellence in rehabilitation training that the subjects received as part of their program." SO THE 1957 FINDING IS 1:8, AND THE FIELD OWN 1979 ASSESSMENT IS THAT IT IS UNREPRESENTATIVE BECAUSE THE SAMPLE WAS ELITE-TRAINED. That is a selection-bias critique published eight years before the ADA, and it is itself first-class historical evidence about how the threshold literature was weighed. A WEB SEARCH SUMMARY ASSERTED THIS WAS A UNIVERSITY OF IOWA MASTER THESIS AND NONE OF ITS OWN RETURNED LINKS SUPPORTED THAT — the claim is discarded, not recorded (CLAUDE.md 5(c)). The institution is NOT established: REF-01005 says only "a pioneering rehabilitation-education center" and naming one would be invention. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW OWN RETRIEVAL CLAIM. The re-description above states "Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0". GOOGLE BOOKS 0 IS FALSE. That query returned HTTP 429 (daily quota exceeded) and was never asked; recording an unfired query as a null is the error batch 17 made on SafetyLit and is corrected here rather than left standing. THE OTHER THREE ZEROS HOLD and were re-derived, not assumed: Open Library numFound 0, Internet Archive 0 relevant, ERIC 0. EVERYTHING ELSE IN THE ROW IS UNAFFECTED — Elmer 1957 finding of 1:8 maximum, and Steinfeld 1979 dismissal of it as an elite-trained sample, come from REF-01005 page 163 and stand on a retrieved primary source. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 RE-DESCRIPTION FROM A PRIMARY SOURCE, AND THE ROW GAINS AN AUTHOR IT NEVER HAD. Staged from REF-00980''s Crossref reference list as a bare title with a year, ''A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps (1957)''. REF-01005 supplies three things the reference list did not, from two different places in the same document. (1) THE AUTHOR AND THE FORM, from its bibliography at pypdf page index 167: ''Elmer, C.D. [A s]t[udy] to determine the s[p]e[ci]fications of wheelchai[r] ramp[s]. Ph.D. d[is]ser[ta]tion, University o[f] Il[linois]''. NOTE HONESTLY: the bibliography''s own year digits are OCR-damaged and read ''194-5/'', which is NOT 1957 and is not resolvable from those bytes. (2) THE YEAR, from the body text at page index 162, which reads ''Elmer (1957)'' cleanly -- so 1957 is corroborated by a primary source, but by the prose, not by the reference entry. (3) THE FINDING, which is why this row now matters more than it did: REF-01005 reports that ''Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users'' and then DISCOUNTS IT ON SAMPLING -- his subjects were young, highly trained users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education centre, where Walter''s sample and Steinfeld''s own included large proportions of older people and many with reduced arm function and low stamina. Captured as extraction 57 with contested=1. WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE ROW''S VALUE: staged as a hypothesis that the oldest ramp-specification study might be the origin of a threshold still in force. It is not that. Its figure is 1:8, which no code carries, and the 1979 report set it aside forty-seven years ago for a reason this project would give today. THE HISTORICAL TRAJECTORY IS THE FINDING: 1:8 (1957, discounted as unrepresentative) -> 1:12 (ANSI, measured and found wanting in 1979) -> 1:16 (Walter 1971 and Steinfeld 1979 converge) -> 1:20 (every wheelchair user in Steinfeld''s sample completed 40 feet). THE PARAMETER HAS MOVED IN ONE DIRECTION, GENTLER, FOR SEVENTY YEARS, AND EACH STEP WAS DRIVEN BY WIDENING THE SAMPLE. NOT RETRIEVED AND STILL OWED as a document: a 1957 Illinois PhD dissertation. ProQuest Dissertations and the University of Illinois institutional repository are the routes and neither has been tried. NOT AN ANCHOR IF RETRIEVED: owner ruling 2026-09-18 places pre-1990 work as historical grounding. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION, TWO PARTS, ONE OF WHICH OPENS A CONTEST THIS ROW MUST NOT CLOSE. (1) THE YEAR WAS ALWAYS PRINTED. My earlier re-description said the bibliography year digits read "194-5/" and were "NOT resolvable to 1957", and that 1957 came only from the body text. The rendered page reads "Elmer, C.D. A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps. Ph.D. dissertation, University of IL, 1957." Author, form, institution and year are all printed in the reference entry. The elaborate distinction I drew between what the bibliography could support and what the prose could support was an artefact of reading a damaged text layer instead of the page. (2) THE AUTHOR IS NOW CONTESTED BETWEEN TWO PRIMARY SOURCES, and this is new evidence rather than a correction of mine. Templer FHWA-RD-79-3 (candidate 125) cites the same 1957 Illinois dissertation as "DIXON, Charles E. A study to determine the Specifications of Wheelchair Ramps, Unpublished Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Illinois, Champagne, Urbana, 1957", and describes its method -- a campus questionnaire, then an adjustable laboratory ramp at 1:8.6 to 1:5, 61 subjects, 30 feet in a single run or two 15-foot runs with a landing. Steinfeld bibliography plainly prints Elmer, C.D. TWO 1979-80 FEDERAL REPORTS DISAGREE ON THE AUTHOR OF THE SAME DISSERTATION. Neither is corrected from the other and NEITHER IS PREFERRED HERE. My earlier note said this row "gains an author it never had"; it gains two, and that should not be read as settled. THE FINDING THE ROW CARRIES IS UNAFFECTED: whoever wrote it, REF-01005 reports its 1:8 result and discounts it on sampling, and FHWA independently reports 61 subjects who were university students, which is the same objection from a second reader. RESOLUTION: ProQuest Dissertations or the University of Illinois repository, searched on TITLE rather than author, since the author is exactly what is in doubt. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): EXHAUSTED FOR NOW, AND NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, applied literally: ''if we can''t access something from anywhere then we have to give up on it for now.'' Full text of the 1957 University of Illinois dissertation is not reachable from anywhere this project can go. ROUTES RECORDED AS TRIED in this row and in batches 17-19''s retrieval logs: Open Library 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0; Google Books refused on quota and never asked. The 2026-09-25 read-only investigation relayed to batch 20 reports re-checking every legitimate self-service route and finding none that works; its requests were not persisted in this repository''s retrieval log, so they are not itemised here. THE EARLIER NEXT STEP IS WITHDRAWN: batch 19 named two repository routes as ''the routes''; under the ruling this row is not a task for any person, human or agent. WHAT STANDS: the 1:8 finding and its 1979 discount on sampling (REF-01005 extraction 57), and the AUTHOR CONTEST, now read on rendered pages of both sources: REF-01005''s bibliography prints ''Elmer, C.D.''; REF-01008''s reference list (printed page 299, transcribed from the render and persisted) prints ''Dixon, Charles E. A study to determine the Specifications of Wheelchair Ramps, Unpublished Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Illinois, Champagne, Urbana, 1957.'' REF-01008 also gives the method as it read it -- a campus questionnaire, then an adjustable laboratory ramp from 1:8.6 to 1:5, 61 subjects, all university students, a 30 ft run or two 15 ft runs with a landing, a five-point scale; recommended ''A maximum gradient of 1:6 ... and a minimum of 1:8.6'', while at 1:6 47 percent rated it hard or very hard. Neither author spelling is preferred. The contest stays open because the document that would settle it is the one given up on. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): DISPOSITION MOVED TO EXHAUSTED. Owner ruling 2026-09-25, recorded on contact (CLAUDE.md rule 0): a real, CHECK-permitted value for ''exhausted, given up for now'' was added (schema migration 096) and applies to this row, to which no route exists from anywhere. The routes tried and the reasons are in the re-description above; this changes the typed column so that no sweep reads PENDING-VERIFICATION as work. Not queued for anyone.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 110;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "suggested_slug" = 'stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility', "notes" = 'SURFACED WHILE FAILING TO FIND SOMETHING ELSE, which is the honest provenance. Crossref, queried on the dissertation title REF-01005''s bibliography carries, returned John A. Templer''s indexed corpus instead: this 1978 NBS report, ''The Forgiving Stair'' (1984, Human Factors Society, doi 10.1177/154193128402800118), and ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'' (1989, doi 10.1518/107118189786758148). Full author list here: John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis. WHAT MAKES IT CHEAP TO RESOLVE: NBS is now NIST and its internal reports are public, so unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this one is very likely retrievable in full without document delivery -- nvlpubs.nist.gov is the route and it has not been tried. IF RETRIEVED, THE QUESTION TO ASK IS NARROW: does it contain any rampway slope or length recommendation at all? If yes, footnote c probably means this and the 1:8/1:10 warrant is identified. If no -- which is what its title predicts -- then footnote c''s Templer is the 1974 dissertation or an item outside every index reached, and either way the warrant for those two bands rests on stair research. THAT QUESTION IS WORTH ONE RETRIEVAL AND NOT MORE. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION: THE PREMISE FOR STAGING THIS ROW IS GONE. It was staged as "the nearest RESOLVABLE item to Table 13 footnote c" on the reasoning that John A. Templer had no ramp research and that a 1978 stairs report was therefore the closest thing to the cited 1977 item. That reasoning is retracted on exec 90: Templer FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3 is dedicated ramp research and is candidate 125. THIS ROW IS NOW WHAT IT ALWAYS WAS ON ITS FACE -- a study of STAIR users, off parameter 3, with no claim to make about ramp gradient. Keep it PENDING rather than retiring it: NBS IR 78-1554 is also free on Internet Archive (identifier analysisofbehavi7815temp), it is by the same author in the same period, and if the FHWA trail fails to identify footnote c referent this is still a node worth reading. But it is no longer the first call, and the question this row was staged to answer should be put to candidate 125 instead. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): REHOME TO stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility (an ACTIVE slug in the slugs table), NOT ADMITTED ON PARAMETER 3. Retrieved in full from NIST (nbsir78-1554.pdf, persisted) and read -- the text layer is sound, and the low-text pages were checked. It is what its title says and what this row''s own staging note predicted: a behavioural and accident study of STAIR users for the Consumer Product Safety Commission, with performance statements on risers, treads, handrails, lighting and visual orientation. It contains NO RAMP SLOPE OR LENGTH RECOMMENDATION: every ''ramp'' is incidental (scope, alternatives to stairs, not mistaking a stair for a ramp, falls on stairs versus falls on ramps, and citations), and every ''gradient'' is the report''s ''orientation gradient'', a perceptual measure, not a slope. R15 CORRECTIONS: the staged title gives 1978; the title page reads ''February 1976, Issued November 1978'' -- the report was written two years before it was issued. The staging premise (nearest resolvable item to footnote c''s ''Templer, 1977'') was already retracted on 2026-09-20 in favour of candidate 125, now REF-01008. WHAT THE READ ADDS TO THE FOOTNOTE-c QUESTION, WITHOUT ANSWERING IT: the report cites ''Templer, J., Stairs and Ramps, Dowden, Hutchinson and Ross, Stroudsburg, PA., forthcoming'' six times -- a Templer book on ramps announced in a 1976-78 federal report. That is a third possible referent for ''Templer, 1977'' beside the 1974 dissertation and the FHWA ramp study, and it is NOT chosen here (candidate 115 stays PENDING). Rehome, not out-of-scope: stair-user behaviour, risers, treads and handrails are that slug''s subject, and a later pass there should read it as primary stair evidence. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): CORRECTION TO THE RE-DESCRIPTION ABOVE, SAME BATCH. It says ''the report was written two years before it was issued''. The title page prints ''February 1976'' and ''Issued November 1978''; it does not say what the earlier date marks (drafting, contract completion, a period covered). Recorded as printed, without the inference. Everything else in the rehoming stands. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; suggested_slug accessible-circulation-geometry -> stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility): TYPED COLUMN BROUGHT INTO LINE WITH THE REHOMING. The re-description above rehomed this row to stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility while suggested_slug still named accessible-circulation-geometry, where it was found -- prose and column disagreeing, found by an independent adversarial pass (finding #5). resolve-candidate could not write suggested_slug; the flag was added in batch 20 (GAP-045) and is used here. The same pass confirmed the content call: ''gradient'' in this report is always an orientation gradient, and there is no ramp recommendation.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 117;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'harm_finding=1: the title poses its own question as whether disabled people are being DENIED rights, so it is an inadequacy study by construction, and R7 makes that first-class evidence rather than a by-product. WHY THIS CLUSTER MATTERS MORE THAN ANY SINGLE ITEM: REF-01006''s reference list contains at least five accessibility-audit studies of Indian and Brazilian health facilities (bb0030 Pinto 2021 Brazil national audit doi 10.3390/ijerph18062953; bb0100 Singh 2024 New Delhi hospitals doi 10.32388/M7E7KE.3; this one; bb0110 Sharma 2025 Gwalior; bb0130 Mohapatra 2024 Delphi on public buildings doi 10.12688/f1000research.156920.1). If any of them tabulates measured gradients, the corpus gains something it has almost none of: what ramps in the world ACTUALLY measure, as against what codes require or what laboratory studies test. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): NOT ADMITTED: THE BODY COULD NOT BE READ FROM THIS ENVIRONMENT, AND THE PREMISE THAT IT WAS OPEN ACCESS AND RETRIEVABLE IS CORRECTED. Batch 19 and the batch-20 runbook both described this as ''DOI-bearing, open access, retrievable''. It is FREE TO READ at PubMed Central (PMC11116980; OpenAlex and Unpaywall both call it green OA with PMC as the only location) but NOT OPEN-ACCESS LICENSED: NCBI efetch returns front matter only, carrying the comment ''The publisher of this article does not allow downloading of the full text in XML form'', and its pmc-prop-open-access flag is ''no''. ROUTES TRIED THIS BATCH, EACH PERSISTED IN THE RETRIEVAL LOG: Crossref record (200, metadata and a 16-entry deposited reference list); Europe PMC core record (200, abstract); Europe PMC fullTextXML (HTTP 500); NCBI efetch db=pmc (200, front matter only, XML withheld by publisher); pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov article page and its /pdf/ (200 but a reCAPTCHA ''Checking your browser'' interstitial, no article); Europe PMC PDF render (403, Cloudflare ''Just a moment''); ScienceDirect PII S0377123722001952 (403, Cloudflare error 1000); Unpaywall and OpenAlex (200, no location other than PMC). WHAT THE ABSTRACT SAYS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT: a 30-day hospital-based cross-sectional audit of one health care institution of national importance in India against the Central Public Works Department (CPWD) checklist -- ''Out of total 126 pointers, 45 pointers were found to be totally compliant, 30 pointers were found to be partially compliant, and remaining 51 pointers were found to be not applicable''. IT DOES NOT SAY whether a ramp gradient is one of the 126 pointers or how any ramp scored, so the staged claim that this document type ''measures ramp gradients in a table'' is UNTESTED for this item, not confirmed. harm_finding=1 stands on the abstract''s own conclusion (''important gaps in accessibility''). NOT QUEUED FOR ANYONE: the text is readable in an ordinary browser at PMC, which is a different situation from the three items the owner ruled exhausted on 2026-09-25, so this row is left PENDING rather than marked exhausted -- but no retrieval task is assigned to any person. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING-VERIFICATION, DELIBERATELY, NOW THAT EXHAUSTED EXISTS (migration 096, owner ruling 2026-09-25). EXHAUSTED is for an item to which no route exists from anywhere. This one has a route: PubMed Central serves it free to read (Unpaywall and OpenAlex both give is_oa true, green, via PMC), and what stopped batch 20 -- a reCAPTCHA interstitial and Cloudflare 403s -- is a barrier to this environment''s automated requests, which may be transient. The owner''s ruling drew exactly this distinction. Not queued for anyone, and no request is drafted.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 123;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'IT MAY ANSWER TABLE 13 FOOTNOTE c, AND THE COINCIDENCE IS STRIKING RATHER THAN CONCLUSIVE. Steinfeld''s Table 13 declares four allowable bands -- 1:8, 1:10, 1:12, 1:16 -- and marks the first two ''Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walters, 1971)''. Templer''s Table 20 makes recommendations over THE SAME FOUR GRADIENTS. NOT GUESSED, per batch 18 section 9 item 3: Volume 3 is dated May 1980 and footnote c says 1977, so this cannot be the cited item as printed; the contract''s field tests ran Jul 1976 to Sep 1977, which makes an INTERIM REPORT of the same project a plausible referent, and that is a hypothesis to test by retrieval rather than a conclusion. IT ALSO CARRIES WALTER 1971 SECOND-HAND, which bears on GAP-026: its literature review describes Walter''s adjustable laboratory ramp in detail -- 20 feet at gradients 1:8.6 to 1:16, 10 feet at 1:5.5 to 1:10, four-point rating scale -- so a document this project CAN retrieve reports the study it cannot. That is corroboration of Steinfeld''s account of Walter by an independent reader, not a substitute for Walter itself. AND IT CONTESTS CANDIDATE 110''s AUTHOR: its reference list cites the 1957 Illinois dissertation as ''Dixon, Charles E.'', where Steinfeld''s bibliography plainly prints ''Elmer, C.D.''. Two primary sources disagree; neither is corrected from the other. RETRIEVAL IS FREE AND THE ROUTE IS PROVEN: archive.org/download/provisionsforeld00temp_0/provisionsforeld00temp_0_djvu.txt. Unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this needs no document delivery. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01008): ADMITTED AS REF-01008, T3 GREY (owner ruling 2026-09-18: pre-1990 work is historical grounding, the REF-01005 treatment). Scan PDF (db21e48738335d82.pdf, 334 images) and DjVuTXT persisted from Internet Archive provisionsforeld00temp_0 (CC BY-NC-ND 3.0, National Transportation Library); every table extracted was RENDERED AND READ, not taken from the text layer. R15 CORRECTIONS TO THE STAGED ROW: (1) AUTHOR. Staged as ''Templer, John A. et al.''; the Technical Report Documentation Page (rendered, index 2) names ONE author, John A. Templer -- the research associates and assistants are credited as the project team, not as authors. Filed as a sole author. (2) WHAT TABLE 20 IS. Staged as ''a recommend/not-recommend matrix over curb heights 3, 6 and 9 inches against gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16'', which is right, and it has a consequence the staged row did not draw: it is a CURB-RAMP table whose recommendation is RISE-CONDITIONED -- 1:8 up to a 3-inch curb, 1:10 up to 6 inches, 1:12 up to 9 inches, steeper than 1:8 never, with the footnote ''Whenever possible slopes less than the maximum should be employed'' -- and its own conclusion is ''steeper ramps are acceptable if they are short''. Two of its cells (1:10 at 6 in, 1:12 at 9 in) were not tested; they interpolate between tested ramps. (3) THE SAMPLE. 120 volunteers, August-November 1976, in eight groups; the 18 manual wheelchair users are young (at least 16 aged 16-35). (4) THE INSTRUMENT. A four-point subject difficulty rating plus an independent tester rating; heart rate and oxygen consumption were considered and rejected. WHAT STANDS: the Walter 1971 account at second hand (printed p.4) -- now extraction 64 -- and the ''Dixon, Charles E.'' attribution of the 1957 dissertation (reference 1), which keeps candidate 110''s author contest open. FOOTNOTE c: this report''s test ramps 9 (1:8, 2 ft, 3 in) and 6 (1:10.67, 8 ft, 9 in) have EXACTLY the run and rise of REF-01005 Table 13''s two footnote-c rows, and ramp 6 was unacceptable to manual wheelchair users in ascent (extraction 66). That is consistent with footnote c''s ''Templer, 1977'' being this project''s ramp work, NOT an identification: this volume is dated May 1980. Candidate 115 stays PENDING. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01008): CORRECTION, SAME BATCH (independent adversarial pass, finding #21): ''334 images'' above is Internet Archive''s imagecount metadata. The persisted PDF (db21e48738335d82.pdf) has 332 pages, and every page index batch 20 cites is a zero-based index into that PDF. ALSO: the untested Table 20 cells are the report''s recommendations, not measurements -- ''interpolate'' above is batch 20''s inference, not the source''s word (see extractions 61 and 62).' WHERE "candidate_id" = 125;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'WHAT IT SAYS ON RAMPS, VERBATIM FROM THE PERSISTED FULL TEXT: method -- ''For long ramps (over 2 m), 2-3 measurements were taken at various points along the length, and the steepest angle was recorded''; finding -- ''of three facilities with ramps, all reached the minimum required width and had textured, non-slip floors but two were too steep and none had handrails, meaning all failed the overall accessibility criteria for a ramp.'' Five facilities audited, three had ramps. R7: a failure finding. Same cluster as candidates 123/124 (health-facility audits in LMICs) and REF-01006, and the second participatory-audit design found in two batches. NEXT: retrieve Supplementary File Appendix 2 from the publisher (BMC additional files) and read the ramp indicator''s slope criterion. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): CORRECTION TO THIS ROW''S WARRANT DESCRIPTION (independent adversarial pass, finding #13). It says the audits were co-facilitated by youth researchers with disabilities, as though that settled the Co-1 question for this cell. The persisted text: the eight youth researchers who adapted the tool were ''themselves all Ugandan healthcare users with disabilities''; ''Six of the eight youth researchers were selected to participate in the pilot as trainee facilitators''; ''Each pair of trainee facilitators included one youth researcher with a disability and one peer without a disability. One trainee was Deaf, one lived with albinism, and one had visual impairment.'' NO MOBILITY IMPAIRMENT IS RECORDED among the facilitators. For staging against parameter 3 x MOB that matters: whatever Co-1 warrant the co-production carries under D-0178, it is not co-production by the population this parameter serves.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 126;

