lier in the same batch; corrected before emission. This row''s claim_text is a TRANSCRIPTION of table cells read off a rendered page and carries a VERBATIM-EXEMPT warrant, so nothing byte-checks it. The corpus''s other exempt transcriptions (REF-01005, extractions 52-57) are ''reviewed'', and ''verified'' is kept here for rows whose claim_text verifies byte-for-byte against a persisted artefact (63, 64).) || 2026-09-25 root_type SET measurement_primary -> committee_assertion (RETYPED measurement_primary -> committee_assertion (independent adversarial pass, finding #2). The notes above say this cell was NOT TESTED (no 1:10 ramp over a 6-in curb exists in Table 1), so the typed root claimed a measurement the prose denies -- the typed-versus-prose contradiction batch 19 corrected on extraction 57. The value is the report''s recommendation for a combination it did not test: an assertion by its authors, self-rooted in REF-01008. committee_assertion is the live vocabulary''s value for a source that asserts rather than measures; derived_calculation would claim a computed value with derived_from edges, and none exists. It also stops the row counting as an independent measured root. ''INTERPOLATION'' IN THE NOTES ABOVE IS BATCH 20''s WORD, NOT THE SOURCE''s: the report never says how its untested cells were set, only ''Based on these findings, Table 20 shows recommendations for maximum acceptable gradients for three different curb heights.'' Read it as an inference. AND THE CELL SITS ON THE WRONG SIDE OF THE REPORT''s OWN LINE: Table 3''s heavy line (''Only ramps to the right of the heavy line are considered acceptable'') runs between 1:10.67 and 1:12 in the 6-in row (render db21e48738335d82.p0036.png), so this recommendation lies on the unacceptable side of the report''s own manual-wheelchair-ascent boundary. ALSO CORRECTED: the batch-20 status note on this row says rows 63 and 64 keep ''verified'' because their claim_text ''verifies byte-for-byte'' against a persisted artefact. It verifies under retrieval_log''s NORMALISED match (letters and digits only), not byte-for-byte: the DjVu text doubles every space, and at extraction 64 reads ''recommended— for self- propelled''.)', "updated_at" = '2026-09-25 06:10' WHERE "extraction_id" = 61;
